 ],
 "literal": "Daylight having passed, jasmine blooming, / the sun's heat having cooled, even the helpless evening / if we swam, with night as the limit, / what indeed, may you live, oh friend, / the flood of night is larger than the ocean!",
 "translation": "As daylight fades and jasmine blooms, even the helpless evening, when the sun's angry heat has cooled, if we have swum through it, taking the night as our limit, what then, my friend? May you live! The flood of night is larger than the ocean!",
 "modern": "பகற்பொழுது கழிய, முல்லை மலர்கள் மலர, கதிரவனின் சினமாகிய வெப்பம் தணிந்த, செயலற்ற மாலைப் பொழுதை, இரவு எல்லையாக அமையுமாறு நாம் நீந்திக் கடந்தோம் என்றால், தோழியே! நீ வாழ்க! அதற்குப் பிறகு வரும் இராக்காலமாகிய வெள்ளம் கடலை விடப் பெரியதே! நாம் அதை எவ்வாறு கடப்போம்?",
 "explanation": "The heroine is speaking to her close friend in a state of deep distress. Her lover has not yet returned, and the arrival of evening—marked by the blooming of the mullai jasmine and the cooling of the sun's rays—intensifies her loneliness. She explains that she has barely managed to survive the painful evening, comparing it to swimming across a body of water with the night as its boundary. However, she despairs because the night itself is a vast, dark flood that is far larger than the ocean. The metaphor of the night as an endless flood beautifully captures her overwhelming grief and helplessness in the mullai landscape of patient waiting.",
